Method of solving this CAT Question from Geometry

Given that a chord of length 5 cm subtends an angle of 60° at the centre of a circle. We have to find the length of a chord that subtends an angle of 120° at the centre of the same circle sin 60° = \(\frac{BC}{OB}\) ⟹ \(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\) = \(\frac{BC}{5}\) ⟹ BC = \(\frac{5\sqrt{3}}{2}\) and AC = \(\frac{5\sqrt{3}}{2}\) So AB = \(\frac{5\sqrt{3}}{2}\) + \(\frac{5\sqrt{3}}{2}\) = 5\sqrt{3} The length of a chord that subtends an angle of 120° at the centre of the same circle is 5\sqrt{3}

The question is "A chord of length 5 cm subtends an angle of 60° at the centre of a circle. The length, in cm, of a chord that subtends an angle of 120° at the centre of the same circle is"

Hence, the answer is 5\sqrt{3}

Choice B is the correct answer.
